    {
      "type": "pullquote",
      "quote": "**\\*A Note on \"Very Depressed\":** *Depressed* is a clinical medical term. Unless you are specifically referring to a clinical mental health condition, words like *despondent*, *down*, *miserable*, or *dejected* are often more accurate and appropriate choices in casual conversation."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "Bonus: 20 Advanced Upgrades Learners Love"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Ready to go beyond the basics? Here are 20 additional high-impact adjective upgrades:"
    },
    {
      "type": "list-item",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        "**Very scary** → *terrifying*",
        "**Very funny** → *side-splitting*",
        "**Very beautiful** → *mesmerizing*",
        "**Very smart** → *gifted*",
        "**Very dirty** → *squalid*",
        "**Very noisy** → *chaotic*",
        "**Very calm** → *serene*",
        "**Very kind** → *compassionate*",
        "**Very polite** → *courteous*",
        "**Very rude** → *obnoxious*",
        "**Very strange** → *bizarre*",
        "**Very obvious** → *evident*",
        "**Very careful** → *meticulous*",
        "**Very lucky** → *fortunate*",
        "**Very unlucky** → *unfortunate*",
        "**Very painful** → *excruciating*",
        "**Very dangerous** → *hazardous*",
        "**Very tasty** → *delicious*",
        "**Very dry** → *arid*",
        "**Very wet** → *soaking / soaked*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "🌐 Multilingual Adaptations for FR, RU, IT & EL Learners"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "In every target language, intermediate learners fall into the habit of relying on a single general intensifier (*très*, *очень*, *molto*, *πολύ*). Here is how to upgrade your adjectives in French, Russian, Italian, and Greek:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "🇫🇷 French (*très* + adjectif → adjectifs forts)"
    },
    {
      "type": "list-item",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**très grand** → *immense, gigantesque*",
        "**très bon** → *excellent, remarquable, formidable*",
        "**très fatigué** → *épuisé, exténué*",
        "**très beau** → *superbe, magnifique, splendide*",
        "**très intéressant** → *passionnant, captivant*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "🇷🇺 Russian (*очень* + прилагательное → сильные прилагательные)"
    },
    {
      "type": "list-item",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**очень большой** → *огромный, гигантский*",
        "**очень хороший** → *отличный, великолепный, превосходный*",
        "**очень усталый** → *изнуренный, обессиленный*",
        "**очень красивый** → *прекрасный, изумительный*",
        "**очень интересный** → *захватывающий, увлекательный*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "🇮🇹 Italian (*molto* + aggettivo → aggettivi forti)"
    },
    {
      "type": "list-item",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**molto grande** → *immenso, gigantesco*",
        "**molto buono** → *eccellente, squisito, ottimo*",
        "**molto stanco** → *esausto, sinito*",
        "**molto bello** → *bellissimo, magnifico, splendido*",
        "**molto interessante** → *affascinante, coinvolgente*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "text": "🇬🇷 Greek (*πολύ* + επίθετο → ισχυρά επίθετα)"
    },
    {
      "type": "list-item",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**πολύ μεγάλος** → *τεράστιος, γιγάντιος*",
        "**πολύ καλός** → *εξαιρετικός, υπέροχος*",
        "**πολύ κουρασμένος** → *εξαντλημένος, κατάκοπος*",
        "**πολύ ωραίος / όμορφος** → *πανέμορφος, καταπληκτικός*",
        "**πολύ ενδιαφέρων** → *συναρπαστικός, καθηλωτικός*"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "Summary: Practicing Strong Adjectives"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "When learning these new words, try substituting them directly into your daily journal or speaking practice. For instance, instead of saying *\"I had a very busy day and I am very tired\"*, say **\"I had a hectic day and I am exhausted.\"** Notice how much more vivid and clear your sentence becomes!"
    }
  ],
